== H.3 “Jealousy, not Iago, destroys Othello.” Discuss.

#text(size: 9.7pt)[
The play gives both sides of the proposition a scene, which is why the question is
worth arguing rather than answering. On Iago's side, jealousy is manufactured in front
of the audience. In the temptation scene he begins with a hesitation — “I like not
that” — then refuses to explain, then warns Othello against jealousy while describing
it precisely enough to produce it, and finally supplies the handkerchief as physical
proof. Nothing in that sequence is spontaneous; it is a technique, and the play shows
the steps. On Othello's side, the technique would not work on a man without a
particular vulnerability. He is a stranger in Venice, a soldier whose eloquence won a
senator's daughter, and a man whose sense of himself depends on being trusted. When
Iago says “I am your own for ever,” Othello answers with a promotion, which shows how
badly he needs a confidant. The play's most economical statement of the balance is
Emilia's: someone was “too fond of her most filthy bargain.” The tragedy needs both the
manipulator and the capacity to be manipulated, and the playwright is careful not to let
either off. Iago supplies the plot; Othello supplies the conditions; and the play's
judgment is that the second is what makes the first possible, which is why Othello's
last speech is an attempt at self-explanation and Iago's is a refusal to explain at all.
]

== H.4 “The handkerchief is the real protagonist of Othello.” Discuss.

#text(size: 9.7pt)[
The claim is a joke with a serious point: the handkerchief has more effect on the plot
than any character except Iago, and the play is careful to give it a biography. It
belonged to Othello's mother, it was given to her by an Egyptian charmer, it carries a
charm that keeps a husband's love constant, and it is a web of strawberries worked by a
sibyl of great age. That history is not decoration. It converts a piece of cloth into a
talisman, so that when Iago plants it in Cassio's lodging the loss becomes evidence of
infidelity rather than carelessness. The play then uses it as a machine with three
moving parts. Desdemona drops it; Emilia picks it up and gives it to Iago; Iago leaves it
with Cassio; Cassio gives it to Bianca to copy; and Othello sees it in Bianca's hand at
the moment Iago has arranged. Each step is small, plausible and irreversible, which is
the definition of a well-made plot device. But the handkerchief is not a protagonist in
the moral sense, because it has no intention. What it exposes is the willingness of the
men around it to treat a domestic object as a proof: Othello asks for it, Iago supplies
it, and neither asks what a handkerchief can actually establish. The play's final scene
confirms the point when Emilia says she found it and gave it to her husband. The cloth
did nothing; the people did everything, and blamed the cloth.
]

