== H.5 How does Faceless show that institutions fail children?

#text(size: 9.7pt)[
Darko's novel answers the question structurally: it moves between the street, the
office and the studio, and each place has a different way of not seeing a child. On the
street, the failure is classification. The body behind the Rasta kiosk is taken for a
kayayoo, a head porter, because that is the category the city has for a poor girl in a
market; the category is accurate and it is also a way of not asking who she was. In the
office, the failure is jurisdiction. MUTE has a director, a programme officer and two
colleagues, and their argument about what can be done is the novel's most honest scene:
Dina can authorise a report, Kabria can insist, and neither can arrest Onko or compel
the police. In the studio, the failure is publicity. Sylv Po broadcasts the story and
Maami Broni telephones; the death becomes a topic, and then the topic becomes old. The
novel is careful not to make any of these people villains. Kabria's persistence is real,
and the novel rewards it with a partial rescue: Fofo is taken in. But Odarley is not,
and the novel ends with that remainder rather than with a solution. The handprint on the
right cheek is the one piece of evidence that crosses the institutional gaps, because it
points to a particular man rather than a category — and even that arrives after the
child is dead. The novel's title is therefore a diagnosis of institutions as much as of
a body: a faceless child is one that no institution has a reason to look at.
]

== H.6 “Lonely Days celebrates a woman's refusal.” Discuss.

#text(size: 9.7pt)[
The novel celebrates the refusal and refuses to celebrate it cheaply, which is why it is
better than a slogan. Yaremi's refusal is prepared for by everything the novel shows
about her: her trade in taffeta, her house, her memory of Ajumobi, and her habit of
speaking to a dead man who cannot answer. When she attends the cap-picking ceremony and
declines to pick, she is not rejecting grief or custom in general. She keeps the
mourning, keeps the house and keeps her husband's name; what she refuses is the
conversion of her widowhood into a transfer of property, and the play of the scene is
that the refusal happens in public, before the suitors and the elders. The novel is
equally clear about the price. The elders threaten her property and her place, and the
ending is not a victory parade: she removes the widowhood garment and resolves to stay,
which is a claim rather than a settlement. What makes the celebration credible is the
three other widows. Dedewe confesses to sins she did not commit, Fayoyin licks the
libation, and Radeke's dirge is called a lie; their compliance measures what Yaremi's
refusal costs in a village where no woman refuses alone. The novel's feminism is
therefore material rather than rhetorical: an income, a house and a daughter's support
are what make the refusal possible, and the loneliness of the title is the price of it.
]

