== H.7 Why does Native Son need two deaths?

#text(size: 9.7pt)[
The novel needs two deaths because it is arguing about intention, and one death cannot
carry the argument. Mary Dalton dies by accident. Bigger is in her bedroom because he
has carried her there, drunk, after a night he did not choose; her blind mother appears
at the door; and he presses a pillow over her face to keep her quiet. The intent is
silence and the result is death, and the gap between them is where the novel's account
of fear lives. Bessie Mears dies by choice. She knows what Bigger has done, she is
frightened, and he decides that she is a risk; he strikes her with a brick and throws
her down an airshaft. The second death is the one that makes him a murderer in the
ordinary sense, and the novel is careful to place it after the reader has been made to
understand the first. The pair also does structural work. Mary's death belongs to Book
One and produces the flight; Bessie's belongs to Book Two and produces the manhunt; and
Book Three turns both into an argument between Max and Buckley about what a life is
worth explaining. Finally the pair exposes the city's scale of attention: Mary's death
becomes a newspaper event, and Bessie's is barely a paragraph. Wright needs both to show
that the same boy can be a victim and a killer, and that the society can see only one
of the two at a time.
]

== H.8 Compare the treatment of justice in Harvest of Corruption and Native Son.

#text(size: 9.7pt)[
Both texts end in a courtroom, and both use the trial to say something about the state
rather than about the accused. In Ogbeche's play, justice arrives late and in portions.
Aloho is dead before the trial begins, and the courtroom's work is to distribute
sentences among the living: twenty-five years for the minister, twenty each for the
judge and the commissioner, ten each for the recruiter and the hotel keeper, five for
the clerk. The distribution is the play's argument, because it measures each
participant's share in a system, and it is made possible by two honest individuals,
Ogeyi and Inspector Inaku, rather than by an institution that reforms itself. In
Wright's novel, the trial is an explanation that ends in an execution. Max's speech
argues that Bigger's crime must be understood as a product of the conditions the court
maintains, and Buckley's reply converts the argument back into a demand for death; the
verdict is never in doubt. The difference is the direction of the judgment. Ogbeche
sentences the powerful and lets the reader feel that the account has been settled, at
least in part. Wright sentences the powerless and lets the reader feel that no account
has been settled at all, because the court has understood nothing. What the two texts
share is a suspicion of the courtroom as a place where truth is produced: in both, the
trial is a performance whose outcome was decided before the evidence was heard, and in
both the most honest characters are the ones who speak outside it — Ogeyi to Yakubu,
and Bigger to Max in the cell.
]

